## 1. Project Title

**ShadowPlay: An Interactive 3D Shadow Sculpting and Optical Illusion Studio**

---

## 2. Abstract

ShadowPlay is an interactive, browser-based 3D application that treats the shadow as the primary artistic and computational output of a scene. A collection of ordinary three-dimensional objects — scattered rods, simple solids or abstract structures — can cast a clear and recognizable two-dimensional silhouette when illuminated from a carefully chosen direction and viewed from a particular perspective.

The application provides a single reusable virtual studio, delivered through **WebGPU** and **TypeScript**, in which users translate, rotate, scale and combine objects, position and colour multiple light sources, and observe real-time shadows projected onto a backdrop. Custom geometry is prepared in **Blender** and exported as **glTF 2.0** for direct loading in the browser. **WebXR** makes the same studio available in an immersive headset view on supported hardware, without a separate build.

Its core functionality comprises interactive object manipulation, dynamic multi-light shadow generation in **WGSL**, a Shadow Puzzle Mode that scores how closely a produced shadow matches a target silhouette using a **GPU compute** pass, an Anamorphic Perspective Illusion mode, a Multi-Light Shadow Studio, and a ShadowArt Creator with capture support. Concepts from computer graphics — geometric transformations, projection, lighting models, shadow mapping, camera transformations, mesh handling, collision detection, animation and stereo rendering — are applied directly to the implementation. WebXR interaction is included as a first-class mode where browser and hardware support permit, with the desktop browser experience as the committed baseline.

---

## 3. Introduction

Shadows are normally treated in computer graphics as a visual cue that grounds objects in a scene and conveys depth, shape and spatial relationships. In traditional art, however, shadows have long been used as a medium in their own right. Shadow-art installations arrange seemingly unrelated or chaotic objects so that a single light source reveals a coherent image on a wall. Anamorphic art similarly depends on a fixed viewpoint, from which a distorted arrangement resolves into a recognizable form.

These works rely on a precise relationship between object geometry, light position and viewing position. Creating them physically requires careful measurement, fabrication and repeated trial and error. A virtual environment removes material constraints and allows the same relationships to be explored instantly and repeatedly.

ShadowPlay uses this opportunity to turn shadow formation into an interactive computer graphics experience. Instead of observing shadows passively, the user constructs them: objects and lights become tools, and the resulting silhouette is the artwork or the puzzle solution. Because the user must reason about how three-dimensional geometry maps to a two-dimensional projection, the application also serves as an intuitive demonstration of projection, lighting and camera concepts studied in the CGVR course.

Running the studio in the browser with WebGPU removes installation and platform friction, lets the same scene run on a laptop, an Android tablet and a headset, and exposes the modern GPU pipeline directly. WebXR extends the same scene into an immersive view, where the user can walk around the projection wall and manipulate objects and lights with tracked controllers.

