## 4. Problem Statement

Projection and shadow formation are typically taught through equations and static diagrams, and in most real-time applications shadows are a by-product of rendering rather than something the user can deliberately shape. Physical shadow art, while compelling, is expensive to prototype, difficult to adjust, and inaccessible to most students and hobbyists.

There is therefore an opportunity for a lightweight, interactive tool that lets users experiment with the geometry of shadows directly: moving objects and lights, observing the projected result immediately, and receiving feedback on how close a shadow is to an intended image. Existing desktop modelling and game engines can produce shadows, but they are general-purpose, require installation and a substantial learning curve, and do not treat the shadow itself as the editable object. WebGL tools run in the browser but expose a higher-level, less explicit rendering model and lack a portable compute path for shadow comparison.

This project addresses that opportunity by developing a focused virtual studio for shadow sculpting and optical illusions, combining a creative sandbox with structured challenges. The aim is not to replace general-purpose modelling or rendering tools, but to provide an engaging and educational environment centred on a single idea: that what is seen in the shadow can differ entirely from the objects that cast it.

