## 6. Proposed Methodology

The project will follow an incremental development approach, in which a working WebGPU core is established first and additional modes are built upon it. The proposed sequence is as follows.

### Phase 1 — Requirements, Design and Asset Planning (Weeks 1–2)

The feature set is finalized and divided among team members. The layout of the virtual studio is designed, consisting of a floor or table area for objects, one or more light mounts, and a projection wall or screen that receives shadows. A library of primitives (cubes, spheres, cylinders, rods, cones, rings) and a small set of custom shapes is planned.

Blender is used to model and export the custom meshes as **glTF 2.0 / GLB**, with attention to clean topology, sensible scale, correct pivot points and a consistent **metre-based, Y-up** convention. The web application architecture is defined: module boundaries, scene state, the WebGPU render loop, asset loading, and a capability check for `navigator.gpu` and `navigator.xr`. A short technical spike validates WebGPU adapter limits and the WebXR/WebGPU path on the target browsers.

### Phase 2 — Studio Environment and Core Interaction (Weeks 2–4)

The project is created with **Vite + TypeScript** and a **Three.js `WebGPURenderer`** as the scene-graph and asset layer; lighting, shadow and scoring pipelines are authored in **WGSL** rather than taken from the engine defaults. The studio scene, a controllable orbit camera, resize handling and a basic HTML/CSS user interface are established.

Object selection by ray casting is implemented, followed by translate, rotate and scale controls through on-screen gizmos and keyboard, mouse or touch input. Grouping, duplication, snapping and alignment aids are added so that users can build composite structures efficiently. Context-loss and device-pixel-ratio handling are addressed early because they affect every later mode.

### Phase 3 — Lighting and Shadow System (Weeks 4–5)

Movable and rotatable point, spot and directional lights are added with controls for position, direction, intensity and colour. Light data is uploaded through uniform buffers or a storage buffer and consumed by WGSL shaders.

Real-time shadows are produced with a custom WebGPU shadow-mapping pipeline: a depth-only render pass per shadow-casting light writes into a `depth32float` texture; a comparison sampler and a percentage-closer filtering (PCF) step reconstruct soft shadow edges; normal-offset bias and slope-scaled bias control acne and peter-panning. Multiple lights share a shadow atlas where practical; point lights use a cube or six-face layout. Resolution, bias and filter radius are tuned so silhouettes on the projection surface are crisp and recognizable. Support for several simultaneous lights is added, and the way overlapping coloured shadows combine is examined and refined.

### Phase 4 — Shadow Puzzle Mode (Weeks 5–6)

Target silhouettes are authored as images. The shadow produced on the projection surface is captured from a dedicated orthographic camera into a render target and compared with the target using **a WGSL compute shader** that builds the intersection and union masks and reduces them to an intersection-over-union (IoU) score. Only the final score is read back to the CPU, avoiding a per-frame GPU stall.

The resulting score drives pass or fail feedback. A limited but polished set of challenges is created across several difficulty levels, varying in the number of available objects, the freedom given over light placement and the tolerance required.

### Phase 5 — Anamorphic Illusion, Multi-Light Studio and WebXR (Weeks 6–7)

In the anamorphic mode, a marked viewing position is defined and the camera can be locked to it or released to explore the scene from other angles, so that the transition between a resolved image and an apparently random arrangement can be demonstrated. In the Multi-Light Studio, coloured lights are used to produce several distinct shadows, which the user may arrange into a combined composition.

The **WebXR** mode is integrated on the same render path: an `immersive-vr` session is requested where supported, a `local-floor` reference space anchors the studio, and per-eye view and projection matrices come from the `XRView` data each frame. Controller `select` and `squeeze` events drive ray-based selection and grab-based manipulation; two-handed input supports scale and rotation. The interface is presented through a WebXR DOM overlay where available, with a compact in-world menu as fallback. Comfort options include seated/standing origin selection, snap turning and a stable horizon. The desktop browser remains fully functional when XR is unavailable.

### Phase 6 — ShadowArt Creator, Polish and Testing (Weeks 7–8)

The free-creation mode is completed with canvas capture (`toBlob`) and saving of compositions as a small JSON scene file. Visual polish is added through materials, light feedback, simple animations and interface refinement.

The application is tested across a browser and device matrix — desktop Chrome/Edge, Android Chrome, and a WebXR-capable headset browser — for usability, frame stability and GPU compatibility. Unit tests cover transform math and the IoU scorer; a Playwright smoke test covers load, scene creation and puzzle scoring. Defects are corrected, and documentation and a demonstration are prepared.

### Optional Phase — Extensions

If time and hardware permit, WebXR hand tracking, `immersive-ar` passthrough, haptic feedback, animated shadows or additional effects may be explored. These are not part of the committed scope.

