## 8. Computer Graphics and Virtual Reality Concepts Involved

| Concept | Practical Use in ShadowPlay |
|---|---|
| **3D geometric transformations** (translation, rotation, scaling) | Every user action on an object is a transformation expressed through model matrices and uploaded to WebGPU uniform buffers. Composite structures rely on hierarchical transformations, in which child objects inherit those of their parent group. Small changes in rotation or position directly alter the resulting shadow shape. |
| **Projection transformations** | A shadow is the projection of 3D geometry onto a surface from the light's point of view. A point light behaves like a perspective projection, whereas a directional light behaves like a parallel (orthographic) projection. An orthographic camera facing the projection wall captures the 2D shadow for puzzle evaluation. WebGPU's clip-space depth range of 0–1 is handled explicitly in the projection matrices. |
| **Lighting models and light-source properties** | Point, spot and directional lights are provided with adjustable position, direction, intensity and colour. Illumination follows a standard model with ambient, diffuse and specular terms implemented in WGSL. Coloured lights allow overlapping shadows to be distinguished and blended in the Multi-Light Studio. |
| **Shadow mapping and real-time shadow generation** | A depth-only WebGPU render pass is recorded for each shadow-casting light, and the resulting depth texture is sampled with a comparison sampler and PCF filtering to determine shadowed regions. Resolution, bias, normal offset and filter radius influence silhouette sharpness and are tuned for clarity. This is the central rendering mechanism of the application. |
| **Camera transformations and perspective projection** | The view and projection matrices govern what the user sees. The anamorphic mode relies on a fixed viewpoint and perspective projection, in which objects at different depths align into one image only from that position. Orbit, zoom and viewpoint-lock controls allow the illusion to be revealed and broken. |
| **Stereo rendering and WebXR view transforms** | In WebXR, the headset supplies a separate view and projection matrix for each eye, plus the reference-space transform for the head pose. The same scene is rendered twice per frame into the XR GPU sub-images, with correct interpupillary distance and a stable `local-floor` origin. |
| **Mesh modelling and geometric object manipulation** | Custom shapes are modelled in Blender with attention to vertex data, normals, scale and pivot, exported as glTF 2.0, and loaded as GPU vertex and index buffers. The shape of a mesh determines its silhouette, so geometry choices influence the available shadows. |
| **Collision detection and object interaction** | Ray casting supports object selection and gizmo interaction on desktop and through WebXR controller rays. Bounds checks prevent objects from overlapping unrealistically, support snapping and alignment, and keep objects and lights within the studio limits. |
| **GPU compute for image comparison** | A WGSL compute shader builds the intersection and union of the rendered shadow mask and the target silhouette, reduces them with workgroup shared memory, and writes a single IoU value to a storage buffer for readback. This is a direct application of parallel reduction and GPU memory hierarchies. |
| **Animation and dynamic visual effects** | Smooth transitions are used for camera movement, viewpoint locking and feedback upon puzzle completion, driven by `requestAnimationFrame` on desktop and `XRFrame` in immersive mode. Light intensity and colour changes provide dynamic visual feedback. Animated shadows are treated as an optional extension. |

