## 9. Proposed Technology Stack

| Component | Role in the Project |
|---|---|
| **WebGPU** | Primary rendering API: device and adapter setup, render pipelines, depth passes, render targets, buffer uploads and GPU compute dispatch. |
| **WGSL** | Shader language for lighting, shadow sampling and PCF, shadow-mask composition and the IoU compute reduction. |
| **Three.js (`WebGPURenderer`) + TSL** | Thin scene-graph, math, glTF loading and input layer. Shadow, lighting and scoring pipelines are authored in WGSL rather than relying on engine defaults. |
| **TypeScript** | Application logic, state management, input handling, object manipulation, puzzle validation and UI behaviour. |
| **Vite** | Development server, hot reload, TypeScript build and static production bundle. |
| **WebXR Device API + WebXR Input Sources** | `immersive-vr` sessions, reference spaces, per-eye views, controller poses, `select`/`squeeze` events and comfort options. |
| **Blender + glTF 2.0 (GLB)** | Modelling and preparation of custom 3D objects, exported with clean topology, scale and pivots for direct browser loading. |
| **HTML / CSS (DOM overlay)** | Desktop interface and WebXR DOM overlay, keeping UI accessible and easy to iterate. |
| **Vitest + Playwright** | Unit tests for transform math and scoring; browser smoke tests for load, scene creation and puzzle evaluation. |
| **Static HTTPS hosting** | Deployment as a link-openable web application; no native installer. |
| **Optional: WebXR Layers, WebXR Hand Input** | Composition layers for sharper XR rendering and markerless hand interaction, if supported by the target device. |

**Target platform:** modern desktop and Android browsers with WebGPU; immersive WebXR on supported headsets. Input: keyboard and mouse, touch, and XR controllers.

### 9.1 Stack Migration Summary

| Layer | Previous stack | Rewritten stack | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| Application runtime | Unity desktop build | Static WebGPU web application | No install, link-based delivery, cross-platform |
| Language | C# | TypeScript | One language for logic, UI and tooling |
| Rendering | Unity built-in renderer | WebGPU + custom WGSL pipelines | Explicit control of passes, bind groups and formats; direct CGVR learning |
| Shadows | Unity shadow mapping | Depth-only WebGPU passes, comparison sampler, PCF, shadow atlas | The shadow algorithm becomes the project's own implementation |
| Scene and assets | Unity scene + FBX/Unity import | Three.js scene graph + glTF 2.0 / GLB | Open asset format, browser-native loading |
| Puzzle scoring | CPU pixel comparison | WGSL compute reduction (IoU) | Parallel GPU evaluation, minimal readback |
| UI | Unity UI | HTML/CSS overlay + WebXR DOM overlay | Fast iteration, accessible, works in XR |
| Immersive mode | Optional VR integration | WebXR `immersive-vr` + input sources | Same scene and code path, headset access from the browser |
| Deployment | Desktop executable | Static HTTPS bundle (Vite build) | Any modern browser, easy demo and evaluation |

### 9.2 Why WebGPU and WebXR

- **Direct access to the graphics pipeline.** Pipelines, bind groups, texture formats, depth passes and compute dispatches are explicit, so projection, lighting, shadow mapping and reduction are implemented rather than hidden behind an engine.
- **Compute for the puzzle loop.** WebGPU compute shaders evaluate the shadow mask against the target silhouette in parallel and return a single score, which is a practical introduction to GPU reduction.
- **One scene, multiple surfaces.** The same studio runs on a laptop, an Android tablet and a headset; WebXR adds an immersive view without a second codebase.
- **Low-friction delivery.** A URL replaces an installer, which simplifies classroom demonstration, evaluation and sharing.
- **Trade-off.** Browser and headset support for WebGPU and for WebXR/WebGPU composition is still uneven. The project therefore commits to a desktop WebGPU baseline and delivers the WebXR mode on a verified browser/headset pair, with capability detection and a clear fallback message rather than a silent failure.

### 9.3 Browser and Device Targets

| Target | Expected role |
|---|---|
| Desktop Chrome / Edge (WebGPU enabled) | Primary development and demonstration baseline |
| Android Chrome | Touch interaction and mobile GPU validation |
| WebXR-capable headset browser (e.g. Quest Browser) | `immersive-vr` mode and controller interaction, where the WebXR/WebGPU path is available |
| Firefox / Safari | Best-effort; WebGPU availability varies by version and platform |

