## 11. Conclusion

ShadowPlay explores a simple but engaging idea: that the shadow of an arrangement of objects can reveal an image entirely different from the objects themselves. By placing shadow formation under the user's control, the project brings together geometric transformations, projection, lighting, shadow mapping, camera behaviour, mesh handling, collision detection, GPU compute and stereo rendering within one coherent application.

The stack has been rewritten around **WebGPU** and **WebXR**: TypeScript and a thin Three.js scene layer replace Unity and C#, WGSL pipelines replace engine-provided shadows, glTF replaces engine-specific asset import, and a static web build replaces the desktop executable. The scope remains deliberately limited to a single virtual studio and a focused set of modes, with core object manipulation, dynamic lighting and shadow generation given priority, so that the project can be completed to a good standard by a five-member team within about two months.

The combination of a creative sandbox and structured shadow challenges is intended to make the application both a complete interactive CGVR project and an intuitive tool for understanding how light, geometry and viewpoint produce the images we see. WebXR, hand tracking and passthrough AR remain extensions and do not alter the central identity of the project: interactive 3D shadow sculpting, dynamic lighting and optical illusions.

